\title{Qest: Quantile-Based Estimators}
\description{
Quantile-based estimators (Q-estimators) fit a parametric model by
minimizing the integrated loss of quantile regression,
\deqn{\sum_i \int_0^1 w(\tau)\rho_\tau(y_i - Q(\tau \mid x_i, \theta))\,d\tau,}
where \eqn{Q(\tau \mid x, \theta)} is the quantile function of the model
(Sottile and Frumento, 2022). Q-estimators are consistent and asymptotically
normal, are usually more robust than maximum likelihood estimators, and allow
for censored and truncated data.
}
\details{
The main functions are:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=Qest]{Qest()}}: models defined by a user-written quantile function, or by one of
the built-in families \link{Qnorm}, \link{Qgamma}, \link{Qpois} and \link{Qunif};
\item \code{\link[=Qlm]{Qlm()}}: linear models with normal errors (closed-form estimating equations);
\item \code{\link[=Qcoxph]{Qcoxph()}}: proportional hazards models with a piecewise linear baseline
cumulative hazard.
}

The weighting function \eqn{w(\tau)} (e.g., \code{\link[=wtrunc]{wtrunc()}}) can be used to
increase the robustness of the estimators.
}
\references{
Sottile, G. and Frumento, P. (2022). Robust estimation and
regression with parametric quantile functions. \emph{Computational Statistics
and Data Analysis}, 171, 107471. \doi{10.1016/j.csda.2022.107471}
}
